member.example:
Example of a valid use case. The Win32 `GetTickCount()` function is a small performance related function
that reads some global memory and returns the value. In this case, the GC transition overhead is significantly
more than the memory read.

using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
class Program
{
    [DllImport("Kernel32")]
    [SuppressGCTransition]
    static extern int GetTickCount();
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{GetTickCount()}");
    }
}

member.summary:
Used to determine the system a rom is classified as (and thus which core to use) for the times when our romloading autodetect magic can't determine a system.
Keys are file extensions, include the leading period in each, and use lowercase;
values are system IDs, use  for unset (though  should also work, omitting will remove from UI).
